    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d115-a2">

                <pb facs="0212" n="182" sameAs="frus:frus1872p2v5/pg182"/>

                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">*<date calendar="gregorian" when="1857-11-24"
                                ><hi rendition="#small-caps">November</hi> 24, 1857</date>.<seg
                            type="margin">[109]</seg></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7. With reference to your remarks upon the map drawn by “Charles Preuss,” * * I
                    beg you to understand me that I do not bring this map forward as any <hi
                        rendition="#italic">authority</hi> for the line of boundary. * *</p>
                <p>I will at once frankly state how far I am willing to concede, but <hi
                        rendition="#italic">beyond what I now offer I can no further go.</hi> In
                    contemplating your view that all the channels between the continent and
                    Vancouver’s Island, from the termination of the Gulf of Georgia to the eastern
                    termination of the Straits of Fuca, are but a continuation of the channel of the
                    Gulf of Georgia, I see a way by which I can in part meet your views without any
                    gross violation of the terms of the treaty. I am willing to regard the space
                    above described <hi rendition="#italic">as one channel</hi>, having so many
                    different passages through it, and I will agree to a boundary line being run <hi
                        rendition="#italic">through the “middle</hi>” <hi rendition="#italic">of
                        it,</hi> in so far as islands will permit.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
